  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA shortwave will move across the area tonight, bringing light \nprecipitation to the area, and a reinforcing shot of cooler air. \nPrecipitation across most of the area should generally fall in the \nform of rain, but across the higher terrain, snow or a rain/snow mix \nis expected. With the colder air moving into the region, frost \nadvisories and freeze warnings have been issued for much of the \narea, outside of the extreme southern zones. In terms of the \nlowlands, best area for these conditions is SE Ohio, NE KY and Mid \nOhio Valley zones, where better clearing will take place as the wave \ncontinues to trek south and east. Sheltered valleys will be most \nsusceptible to the cold. Monday will also be quite cool as of late, \nwith a northerly flow across the area. \n\nHigh pressure will then build into the region for Monday into \nTuesday, with a very dry air mass taking hold. Afternoon relative \nhumidity both days will bottom out in the 20 to 30 percent range, \nwith pockets of teens not completely out of the question. With the \ndry air mass in place, expecting a clear, cold night for Monday \nnight. A freeze watch has been issued for Monday night/Tuesday \nmorning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAnother shortwave will drop into the area on Wednesday. This will \nprovide the potential for another round of light precipitation to \nthe area, and bring a temporary reprieve to the critically low RH. \nHowever, high pressure and upper ridging will take hold again, with \na return of above normal temperatures, and very dry conditions, with \ncontinued fire weather concerns. However, by the end of the week \ninto the weekend, there are indications of unsettled weather taking \nhold, with chances for showers and storms area wide, and possibly \nsome relief to the overly dry conditions as of late.",
